      \field{abstract}{The most popular {SA} practice seen in the literature is that of ’one-factor-at-a-time’ ({OAT}). This consists of analyzing the effect of varying one model input factor at a time while keeping all other fixed. While the shortcomings of {OAT} are known from the statistical literature, its widespread use among modellers raises concern on the quality of the associated sensitivity analyses. We introduce a novel geometric proof of the inefficiency of {OAT}, with the purpose of providing the modelling community with a convincing and possibly definitive argument against {OAT}. Alternatives to {OAT} are indicated which are based on statistical theory, drawing from experimental design, regression analysis and sensitivity analysis proper.}
