      \field{abstract}{{INTRODUCTION}: We investigated whether sleep features from multi-night, at-home in-ear {EEG} could distinguish mild cognitive impairment ({MCI}) from cognitively normal ({CN}) older adults, independent of demographic confounds. {METHODS}: Forty-three older adults (24 {MCI}, 19 {CN}) completed 361 overnight in-ear {EEG} recordings at home (4.44 ± 2.29 nights per participant). Deep learning-derived embeddings were compared to bandpower features. We controlled for age and sex to identify sleep-specific features of {MCI} beyond demography.}
